+#### 8. Same community, different language: a contrast for §6.2 (new, secondary)
+
+*Why.* In §6.2 language and community coincide: every Paiwan utterance comes from the Paiwan
+community, and so on. A distance effect could reflect language or community. The Mandarin
+utterances of item 1 are spoken mostly by members of the same communities (interviewees in the
+same broadcasts), so they hold community fixed while changing language.
+
+*What is computed.* Units as in item 6, point 2 (labeled, within one shot, at least 8 tight frames,
+kinematic primary of item 6, point 1). For each of the six communities c (the language of the clip):
+queries are the units of c that Gemini judges `mandarin` (not `mixed`); targets are the units of
+each community kept by item 1 as Indigenous-language speech. Units excluded by item 1 only
+through rule (b), or judged `mixed` or `no_speech`, are neither queries nor targets. Retrieval, hit, chance and lift as in
+item 6, point 2. Lift_same(c): targets of c, **excluding units of the query's own clip** (in the same
+clip the Mandarin and the Indigenous-language speaker are often the same person, which would
+measure the person, not the community). Lift_other(c): the mean of the lifts with targets of each
+other community. Statistic: D = the mean over communities of Lift_same(c) minus Lift_other(c),
+two-sided, 95% interval and p from a day-cluster bootstrap (days within language, 10,000
+resamples, `default_rng(20261019)`). A community with fewer than 10 eligible Mandarin units is
+left out of D and reported.
+
+*Read in advance.* D clearly above 0: gesture similarity follows community at least in part, and
+the distance effect of §6.2 cannot be attributed to language alone. D near 0: people of a
+community, speaking Mandarin, gesture no more like their community's Indigenous-language speech
+than like other communities', which is more consistent with gesture following language. D clearly
+below 0 is given no reading in advance and is reported as found. Neither
+reading is a proof: the Mandarin units are mostly interviews and the Indigenous-language units
+include narration and anchors, and the language class comes from a model (error bounded by
+item 1). Reported with D: the 8 framing measures of item 6, point 4 for Mandarin and
+Indigenous-language units side by side; the share of each class in tight frames.
+
+*Sensitivity.* D with same-clip targets included.
